the landscape of massively multiplayer  RPGs has evolved over the last few years  and the audience is followed suit where  we were once used to paying subscription  fees for this type of content  expectations have changed but some  people involved with creating the latest  MMO still think that subscriptions have  a place in the industry today we asked  them for their thoughts on the matter  and why they think the model can still  be relevant  I I don't think that you could take  subscriptions and just sort of liberally  apply it all games and say well these  guys didn't do it well they're there for  it doesn't work anymore  I think each game is unique there have  certainly been examples in recent past  of and on those that try to do a  subscription and they didn't succeed I  think you could have a reasonable  argument amongst reasonable people as to  whether or not the problem in that case  was the subscription or the game and  what I've offered and what it would  whether it was worth the subscription if  we succeed and we're releasing stuff  that people make people say this is  worth my money I got more out of this  fifteen dollars in terms of what I'm  getting to do in this game than I would  have if I had applied that towards any  other game or anything else so it's a  value proposition we feel pretty strong  about the support that we're gonna have  for the game of what you're gonna get  for those dollars and ultimately we  think fans are going to agree  appreciating it's not for everybody but  no game is really for everybody if if  this is the kind of game that you want  to play and you're willing to put down  subscription money then it's an option  if you're straight-up not willing to pay  a subscription then we recognize that  you're not gonna play it but we're not  trying to make a game that like  everybody who plays games will  automatically buy there is a version of  this what we could have said we're not  going to charge a subscription and we  are going to allocate whatever  percentage of the team to make stuff  that we can we can afford a support  based on the base sale of the game but  we decided that's not the that's not  what we want to do like that just seems  like a lesser game and we're not gonna  make a lesser game that that might be  more palatable we want to do the version  of the thing that we think is the best  game and the coolest experience in  contrast MMO wildstar from carbine  studios runs a slightly different  subscription model gametime consumes a  resource named cred which can be  purchased using real world money or in  the game using in-game gold this means  more time you spend playing the game and  gathering gold the more you can extend  your time to play the game to gather  more gold  our intention with cred is that as we  look to the market it seems like the  best way to get some key things that we  wanted firstly obviously people want to  play for free if we could support that  while being responsible financially we  wanted to secondly some folks want to  buy in-game goods for cash they have  extra money they want to put that to  work for them in the game because maybe  they don't have the time to go in and  play for tons of hours we looked at  lineage for instance for every dollar  people paid us in subscriptions the size  of the Korean eBay market said that  about five dollars was spent by other  players buying weapons and castles this  meant that we might make 200 million  dollars on the game but people were  paying over a billion for instance  showing that there was a market there so  the joy of the model is it pairs these  people person B by his cred and trades  it safely on the commodities exchange no  scamming no hacking person Abe buys it  for gold and both players are happy win  and win carbine studios described the  value of the game is being in line with  what players paid for in subscriptions  our intention has been to be as  aggressive with content drops as was  feasible while also supporting a massive  live game we have multiple teams working  on drops as we speak  entire zones worth of quest path and  event content will be released  throughout the year we feel that this  gives us an edge in the MMO space  because we are providing the customer  with the most value not to mention the  most fun for their subscription  final fantasy 14 a realm reborn launched  a PC PlayStation 3 and PlayStation 4  less than 12 months ago like in the  older scrolls online its users pay a  subscription fee but why was this the  best model to go with director and  producer Naoki Ishida explains  it all comes down to the market and the  requirement from the knees from the  players and them are took in a market  currently we are running to a  subscription business model and I think  it's going too smoothly so there's no  immediate plan that you to switch to a  free-to-play model or anything like that  and that being said we are considering  introducing a free trial system which  will allow players to try out the game  for approximately maybe around ten days  for free and if they like it they can  peruse the retail version that kind of  idea we definitely investigated at the  moment I'm not saying that we have to  have the subscription model so maybe in  the future of time war car it all comes  down to if we receive enough but a lot  of requesting players and what we want  to focus in the future but they're the  most important thing is to have the  enjoyable great game and otherwise only  their model would be successful despite  a precedent set by other MMOs switching  to a free-to-play model these games have  managed to make subscriptions work what  do you think could these be the last  major MMOs to launch with a subscription  fee could wildstar be onto something  with players able to pay for time using  in-game currency let us know your  thoughts in the comments below
 

